  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through next Sunday) \nIssued at 1127 PM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nModels continue to trend toward a fairly potent upper-level \ntrough tracking through West Central Texas Tuesday, bringing \nincreased chances for a mix of rain and snow across the area \nthrough early Tuesday evening. Models have also started to come\ninto better agreement with some increased snow potential north of\na Abilene to San Angelo line Tuesday morning. Overall, the best\nchances for snow are expected to remain in the higher elevations\nfrom the central and western Concho Valley and northward into the\nBig Country. At the surface we expect some snow mixing in with \nrain, with rain being the predominant precipitation type. Lows on\nTuesday morning will start off in the mid to upper 30s, with high\ntemperatures reaching into the mid to upper 40s by Tuesday\nafternoon. Given air and ground temperatures will generally \nremain above freezing, and the precipitation is expected to be \nlight, at this time we aren't expected snow to stick well. \nHowever, there still can be some impacts to roadways, so please \nuse caution if driving during this time frame. An SPS will be\nissued to address the light snow Tuesday morning across the\nnorthwest part of the area and colder temperatures this week. \n\nAs the trough moves eastward by Wednesday and high pressure\nattempts to build behind it, a drier forecast begins to take\nshape across the area through the weekend. With the drier \nconditions and less cloud cover, we could see some radiational \ncooling on both Wednesday and Thursday mornings. This will likely \nbring the coldest temperatures for the week, with lows Wednesday \nand Thursday mornings in the upper 20s to low 30s. Freeze warnings\nmay become necessary given these some areas could see \ntemperatures down in the 20s. A gradual warming trend will begin \nWednesday afternoon with high temperatures reaching back into the \nmid 50s for Thanksgiving and then into the 60s to near 70 for the \nweekend.",
